<GuideLayout {title} {description} canonicalPath={path} published="2026-11-23" modified="2026-11-25" {sources}>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#timeline">Which version to target</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#before-you-start">Before you start</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#removed">Removed APIs</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#new-warnings">New deprecation warnings</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#tls">TLS and crypto</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#native">Native addons</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#modules">Modules and globals</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#tooling">Corepack and platforms</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#checklist">Checklist</a>
  </Fragment>

  <p>Node.js 26 has been the Active LTS line since October 28, 2026, and Node.js 22 reaches end of life on April 30, 2027. Moving from 22 to 26 crosses four majors (23, 24, 25 and 26). This guide lists the changes between them that can break a working application, taken from the official changelogs and the Node.js 26 deprecation list, with a quick search for each one.</p>
  <p>The searches use <a href="https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep">ripgrep</a> (<code>rg</code>), which respects <code>.gitignore</code>, so <code>node_modules</code> is usually skipped. They are deliberately simple: they can match code that is fine and miss code that isn't, so treat each result as a place to look, not a verdict. A removed API used inside a dependency won't show up here; updating the dependency is the fix for that.</p>

  <h2 id="timeline">Which version to target</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Status (November 2026)</th><th>End of life</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Node.js 22 "Jod"</td><td>Maintenance LTS since October 21, 2025</td><td>April 30, 2027</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Node.js 24 "Krypton"</td><td>Maintenance LTS since October 20, 2026</td><td>April 30, 2028</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Node.js 26 "Lithium"</td><td>Active LTS since October 28, 2026; maintenance from October 20, 2027</td><td>April 30, 2029</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Node.js 23 and 25 were short-lived Current releases and are already past end of life. Node.js 27 is scheduled for April 22, 2027. Dates come from the official release schedule, which can change; check it when you plan.</p>
  <p>Node.js 26 gives the longest runway: a year more than 24. Choose 24 only if something you depend on doesn't support 26 yet, and plan the second move before April 2028. Either way, the changes below up to the version you choose all apply.</p>

  <h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
  <p>First find every place your project declares a Node.js version. Production runs whichever one the deploy path picks, which is often not the one in <code>.nvmrc</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>cat .nvmrc .node-version 2&gt;/dev/null
rg -n '"node"' -g 'package.json'
rg -n '^FROM\s+\S*node' -g 'Dockerfile*'
rg -n 'node-version' .github/workflows</code></pre>
  <p>Then run your tests on Node.js 22 with deprecations turned into errors. Most of what Node.js 23 to 26 removed already prints a <code>DeprecationWarning</code> on Node.js 22: of the 27 removals listed in the next section, 20 warn at runtime on Node.js 22.23.5. Fixing them while you are still on 22 keeps the upgrade itself small:</p>
  <pre><code>NODE_OPTIONS=--throw-deprecation npm test
# or, to see where each warning comes from without failing:
NODE_OPTIONS=--trace-deprecation npm test</code></pre>
  <p>The Node.js project publishes <a href="https://github.com/nodejs/userland-migrations">official codemods</a> for many of these removals, run with <code>npx codemod@latest @nodejs/&lt;recipe&gt;</code>. The deprecation list links the recipe for each one that has it. Review what a codemod changes the same way you would review a person's change.</p>

  <h2 id="removed">Removed APIs (End-of-Life) between 23 and 26</h2>
  <p>These no longer exist or now throw. The version is the first major without them, so if you stop at Node.js 24, only the rows up to 24 apply.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Removed</th><th>In</th><th>Use instead</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>util.isBoolean()</code>, <code>util.isBuffer()</code>, <code>util.isDate()</code>, <code>util.isError()</code>, <code>util.isFunction()</code>, <code>util.isNull()</code>, <code>util.isNullOrUndefined()</code>, <code>util.isNumber()</code>, <code>util.isObject()</code>, <code>util.isPrimitive()</code>, <code>util.isRegExp()</code>, <code>util.isString()</code>, <code>util.isSymbol()</code>, <code>util.isUndefined()</code></td><td>23</td><td><code>typeof</code>, <code>Array.isArray()</code>, <code>Buffer.isBuffer()</code> and similar checks (codemod <code>@nodejs/util-is</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>util.log()</code></td><td>23</td><td>A logging library, or <code>console.log</code> with your own timestamp</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>process.assert()</code></td><td>23</td><td>The <code>node:assert</code> module</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>zlib.bytesRead</code></td><td>23</td><td><code>zlib.bytesWritten</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tls.createSecurePair()</code>, <code>tls.SecurePair</code></td><td>24</td><td><code>tls.TLSSocket</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>OutgoingMessage.prototype._headers</code>, <code>._headerNames</code></td><td>24</td><td><code>getHeader()</code>, <code>getHeaders()</code>, <code>getHeaderNames()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fs.truncate()</code> with a file descriptor</td><td>24</td><td><code>fs.ftruncate()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>timers.enroll()</code>, <code>timers.unenroll()</code>, <code>timers.active()</code>, <code>timers._unrefActive()</code></td><td>24</td><td><code>setTimeout()</code>, <code>setInterval()</code>, <code>timeout.refresh()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>net._setSimultaneousAccepts()</code></td><td>24</td><td>Nothing; it was an undocumented debugging function</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dirent.path</code></td><td>24</td><td><code>dirent.parentPath</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SlowBuffer</code></td><td>25</td><td><code>Buffer.allocUnsafeSlow(size)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fs.F_OK</code>, <code>fs.R_OK</code>, <code>fs.W_OK</code>, <code>fs.X_OK</code></td><td>25</td><td><code>fs.constants.F_OK</code> and the rest</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fs.rmdir(path, &#123; recursive: true &#125;)</code></td><td>25</td><td><code>fs.rm(path, &#123; recursive: true, force: true &#125;)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>assert.fail()</code> with more than one argument</td><td>25</td><td><code>assert.fail(message)</code> or another <code>assert</code> method</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>assert.CallTracker</code></td><td>25</td><td>Nothing in <code>node:assert</code>; use your test runner's mock functions</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>process.on('multipleResolves')</code></td><td>25</td><td>Nothing; the event is gone</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ChildProcess._channel</code></td><td>25</td><td><code>ChildProcess.channel</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Closing an <code>fs.FileHandle</code> by garbage collection</td><td>25</td><td>Call <code>filehandle.close()</code> explicitly; leaving it to GC now throws</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dns.lookup()</code> with a falsy host name, a TLS <code>servername</code> set to an IP address, <code>url.parse()</code> with a non-numeric port</td><td>25</td><td>Validate the input: these are no longer accepted</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ServerResponse.prototype.writeHeader()</code></td><td>26</td><td><code>writeHead()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>require('node:_stream_wrap')</code>, <code>_stream_readable</code>, <code>_stream_writable</code>, <code>_stream_duplex</code>, <code>_stream_transform</code>, <code>_stream_passthrough</code></td><td>26</td><td><code>node:stream</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Short GCM authentication tags without an explicit <code>authTagLength</code></td><td>26</td><td>Pass <code>authTagLength</code> when you create the decipher</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'util\.(is[A-Z]\w*|log)\(|process\.assert\(|zlib.*bytesRead'
rg -n 'SecurePair|\._headers\b|\._headerNames\b|_setSimultaneousAccepts'
rg -n '\b(enroll|unenroll|_unrefActive)\(|timers\.active\('
rg -n 'SlowBuffer|fs\.[FRWX]_OK\b|CallTracker|multipleResolves|\._channel\b'
rg -n 'rmdir(Sync)?\([^)]*recursive|assert\.fail\([^)]*,'
rg -n '\.writeHeader\(|_stream_(wrap|readable|writable|duplex|transform|passthrough)'
rg -n 'withFileTypes'    # then check for dirent.path
rg -n 'setAuthTag\('     # then check authTagLength on the decipher</code></pre>

  <h2 id="new-warnings">New deprecation warnings in 24, 25 and 26</h2>
  <p>These still work on Node.js 26 but print a warning. They matter now because removal is the next step: on Node's main branch, seven of them are already marked End-of-Life for a future major.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>url.parse()</code></strong> (24): warns when called from your own code, not from <code>node_modules</code>. <code>url.format(string)</code> and <code>url.resolve()</code> call it internally, so they are covered too. Use the WHATWG <code>URL</code> class.</li>
    <li><strong><code>spawn</code> or <code>execFile</code> with an <code>args</code> array and the <code>shell</code> option</strong> (24): the arguments are only space-separated, not escaped, which can lead to shell injection.</li>
    <li><strong>Calling <code>node:zlib</code> classes without <code>new</code></strong> (24), and <strong>passing an unsupported argument type to <code>fs.existsSync</code></strong> (24), which will throw instead of returning <code>false</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>crypto.fips</code></strong> (23): use <code>crypto.setFips()</code> and <code>crypto.getFips()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>ecdh.setPublicKey()</code></strong> (25), and <strong>SHAKE-128/256 digests without an explicit <code>outputLength</code></strong> (25).</li>
    <li><strong>Passing a <code>CryptoKey</code> to <code>node:crypto</code> functions</strong> (26), and <strong><code>KeyObject.from()</code> with a non-extractable <code>CryptoKey</code></strong> (26).</li>
    <li><strong><code>module.register()</code></strong> (26): use <code>module.registerHooks()</code>, which runs synchronously, in-thread, for all module types. Loaders for TypeScript, mocking and instrumentation often call <code>module.register()</code>, so this warning may come from a tool rather than your code.</li>
    <li><strong><code>options.type</code> in <code>Duplex.toWeb()</code></strong> (26): use <code>readableType</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'url\.(parse|format|resolve)\('
rg -n 'shell:\s*(true|["\x27/])'
rg -n 'zlib\.(Deflate|DeflateRaw|Gunzip|Gzip|Inflate|InflateRaw|Unzip)\(' | rg -v 'new '
rg -n 'crypto\.fips|setPublicKey\(|shake(128|256)'
rg -n 'module\.register\(|registerHooks'</code></pre>

  <h2 id="tls">TLS and crypto: security level 2</h2>
  <p>This is the change most likely to pass every unit test and fail in production. Node.js 22 now bundles OpenSSL 3.5 too (since 22.20.0), but it keeps the OpenSSL security level at 1, and its TLS docs state a default of 1. Node.js 24 moved to OpenSSL 3.5 in 24.5.0 without that pin, and the Node.js 24 and 26 docs both give a default of 2.</p>
  <p>OpenSSL defines level 2 as 112 bits of security: <strong>RSA, DSA and DH keys shorter than 2048 bits and ECC keys shorter than 224 bits are prohibited</strong>, and RC4 cipher suites are refused. A 1024-bit RSA key that works on Node.js 22 is rejected on 24 and 26. It can be your own server certificate, a client certificate for mutual TLS, a private CA in your trust store, or a partner's endpoint you call.</p>
  <p>Check the key sizes you control, and test every outbound TLS integration against the target version in staging:</p>
  <pre><code># key size of a certificate
openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -text | rg 'Public-Key'
# certificates and keys checked into the repo
rg --files | rg '\.(pem|crt|cer|key)$'
# places that set ciphers or a security level
rg -n 'SECLEVEL|ciphers:|--tls-cipher-list'</code></pre>
  <p>Node's docs show how to lower the level with <code>@SECLEVEL=0</code> in a cipher string, and warn against doing it globally with <code>--tls-cipher-list</code>. If a partner still needs a weak key, lower it only on that one connection, record it as a known risk with an owner, and plan its removal.</p>
  <p>Node.js 26 also removed short GCM authentication tags without an explicit <code>authTagLength</code> (see the table above). Code that decrypts AES-GCM data produced elsewhere, for example by another service or a mobile client, is where to look.</p>

  <h2 id="native">Native addons: rebuild for ABI 147</h2>
  <p>A native addon compiled against Node's internals only loads on the Node.js version it was built for. That version is <code>process.versions.modules</code> (<code>NODE_MODULE_VERSION</code>): <strong>127</strong> on Node.js 22, <strong>137</strong> on 24 and <strong>147</strong> on 26. A binary built for 127 fails to load on 147, with an error naming both <code>NODE_MODULE_VERSION</code> values.</p>
  <p>Addons built only on Node-API (<code>process.versions.napi</code>) are the exception: Node-API is ABI-stable across Node.js versions, so the same binary keeps working. The package still has to publish a binary for your operating system, CPU architecture and C library, or compile one at install time.</p>
  <p>npm's lockfile marks packages that run an install script, which is usually a native build, with <code>hasInstallScript</code>. Start there, including dependencies of dependencies:</p>
  <pre><code>node -e "const l = require('./package-lock.json'); for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(l.packages ?? &#123;&#125;)) if (v.hasInstallScript) console.log(k)"</code></pre>
  <p>Install and build in the same image that will run in production, on the target version. Don't copy <code>node_modules</code> from a build stage on another Node.js version, or from a developer's machine, into the runtime image.</p>

  <h2 id="modules">Modules and globals</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>require(esm)</code> is on by default</strong> (since 22.12.0 and in 23+). <code>require()</code> of an ES module no longer throws <code>ERR_REQUIRE_ESM</code>; it throws <code>ERR_REQUIRE_ASYNC_MODULE</code> instead if the module or its dependencies use top-level <code>await</code>. Code that caught <code>ERR_REQUIRE_ESM</code> to fall back to <code>import()</code> may now take a different path.</li>
    <li><strong><code>--experimental-transform-types</code> was removed</strong> in 26. Scripts or <code>NODE_OPTIONS</code> that pass it fail to start.</li>
    <li><strong><code>--experimental-permission</code> became <code>--permission</code></strong> in 24.</li>
    <li><strong><code>localStorage</code> is a global, with a catch.</strong> Web Storage is enabled by default since 25. On 25, reading <code>localStorage</code> without <code>--localstorage-file</code> returns an empty object; on 26 it throws a <code>DOMException</code>. Server code that checks for <code>localStorage</code> to decide whether it runs in a browser needs another check. <code>--no-experimental-webstorage</code> turns the API off.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'ERR_REQUIRE_ESM'
rg -n 'experimental-transform-types|experimental-permission'
rg -n 'localStorage'</code></pre>

  <h2 id="tooling">Corepack, npm and platforms</h2>
  <p><strong>Corepack is no longer distributed with Node.js, as of 25.0.0.</strong> A Dockerfile or CI step that runs <code>corepack enable</code> to get pnpm or Yarn now depends on something else providing Corepack: check whether your base image or CI setup installs it. The Corepack project's own instructions are <code>npm install -g corepack</code>. A <code>packageManager</code> field in <code>package.json</code> is a sign the project relies on it.</p>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'corepack' -g 'Dockerfile*' -g '*.yml' -g '*.yaml' -g 'package.json'
rg -n '"packageManager"' -g 'package.json'</code></pre>
  <p>Other changes to check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>npm 11</strong> ships with Node.js 24. Run your install and lockfile checks with the npm version that comes with your target.</li>
    <li><strong>Platforms:</strong> 32-bit Windows was dropped in 23. Node.js 24 raised the minimum supported macOS to 13.5, removed 32-bit s390 and moved armv7 to experimental support.</li>
    <li><strong>Behavior worth a test run:</strong> <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> uses <code>AsyncContextFrame</code> by default from 24 (relevant if you use APM or request-context libraries); <code>fetch</code> runs on Undici 7 in 24 and Undici 8 in 26; the Temporal API is enabled by default in 26.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checklist">Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pick the target (26 unless a dependency blocks it) and change every version declaration together: version files, <code>engines</code>, Dockerfiles, CI and hosting settings.</li>
    <li>On Node.js 22, run the tests with <code>--throw-deprecation</code> and fix what fails.</li>
    <li>Run the searches above and work through the removed APIs and new warnings.</li>
    <li>Check key sizes and test outbound TLS on the target version in staging.</li>
    <li>Rebuild native dependencies in the production image on the target version.</li>
    <li>Install Corepack explicitly if you use it.</li>
    <li>Run the full test suite and a production-like build on the target, then roll out gradually with a way back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="neukee">Where Neukee fits</h2>
  <p>Neukee is an open-source, local-first scanner for upgrades like this one. Version 0.1.0, planned for December 8, 2026, is built to flag Node.js versions that disagree across your repository, versions below your target or past end of life, and dependencies that run install scripts, each linked to its official source. Rules for the removed APIs in this guide are planned after launch. Follow it on <a href="https://github.com/neukee/neukee">GitHub</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
